  1	# SFN-22 migration and rollback
  2	
  3	## What changed
  4	
  5	Super Console now derives its module catalog entirely from the
  6	`AdminCapabilityAdapter` manifests supplied by the installation. The core no
  7	longer exports or consults a fixed module catalog, named nesting table, or
  8	product-specific routes. Any valid lowercase manifest ID can participate in
  9	registry, navigation, generic resource pages, REST/OpenAPI, the generated
 10	client, and MCP discovery.
 11	
 12	`AdminResourceDefinition.presentation` is an optional, validated presentation
 13	hint. It can identify list/detail read operations and configure title,
 14	subtitle, status, columns, formats, and default sort for generic pages.
 15	Authorization, validation, safety, audit, redaction, and confirmation remain
 16	owned by the operation manifest and shared dispatcher.
 17	
 18	Operator authentication in core is provider-neutral. AuthFn composition moved
 19	to `@authfn/admin` as `createAuthFnOperatorAuth`, and provider-selected CSRF
 20	cookie/header names are projected to the browser shell. Default OpenAPI
 21	security scheme keys are now `operatorSession` and `operatorApiKey`.
 22	
 23	## Upgrade
 24	
 25	1. Supply every available function adapter in `adapters` and select deployable
 26	   root manifests in `enabledModules`. Do not import a console catalog.
 27	2. Express required dependencies, folded children, ownership, navigation, and
 28	   display metadata in each function-owned manifest.
 29	3. Replace imports of `createAuthFnOperatorAuth` from `superconsole/server`
 30	   with `@authfn/admin`, or provide another `SuperConsoleOperatorAuth`.
 31	4. Rename custom OpenAPI security scheme entries from `authFnSession` and
 32	   `authFnApiKey` to the required provider-neutral keys `operatorSession` and
 33	   `operatorApiKey`. The scheme values may still describe provider-specific
 34	   cookies or headers.
 35	5. Replace product-only console routes with generic resources/operations and,
 36	   when useful, `resource.presentation`. Application-specific build-time routes
 37	   may live in the host application, outside the core package.
 38	
 39	No stored data migration is required. Existing admin operation IDs and API
 40	routes remain authoritative unless a function manifest itself changes them.
 41	
 42	## Compatibility and rollout
 43	
 44	Roll out the new core and all changed function-owned adapters together. Before
 45	deployment, compare the authorized registry, OpenAPI operation set, client
 46	invocation set, and MCP tool set for the chosen manifests. Disabled or absent
 47	manifests must produce no entries in any of those surfaces.
 48	
 49	The source guard `npm run verify:function-agnostic --workspace=superconsole`
 50	prevents concrete function identifiers and fixed-catalog symbols from being
 51	reintroduced into runtime source.
 52	
 53	## Rollback
 54	
 55	Rollback is code/configuration-only: deploy the previous Super Console and
 56	matching adapter versions, restore the prior installation module and OpenAPI
 57	scheme keys, and remove any `presentation` metadata that the previous
 58	`@superfunctions/admin` validator does not understand. No database reversal is
 59	needed. Do not mix a previous core with newly renamed auth configuration.
 60	
 61	## McpFn boundary decision
 62	
 63	McpFn remains an optional transport integration in the first manifest-driven
 64	delivery. Keeping the bridge beside the shared dispatcher preserves identical
 65	scope, policy, validation, idempotency, confirmation, redaction, and audit
 66	semantics across REST, client, and MCP exposure. Extracting an
 67	`@superfunctions/admin-mcp` package is a compatible follow-up if independent
 68	release cadence or dependency weight justifies it; that package must consume
 69	the same registry/dispatcher rather than create a second authorization path.

